530 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 155 0 R/T(The realistic simulation of crowds is of great importance to the design of safe structures and urban environments. Within structural engineering, crowd behaviour is often overlooked when considering the dynamics of structures such as floors and bridges which are designed to carry human traffic. Predicting the vibration response of lightweight, slender structures to crowds has been an active area of study since the closure of the London Millennium Bridge in 2000 due to uncomfortable vibrations under crowded conditions. There are still gaps in the knowledge in this area, including the exact extent to which gait synchronisation plays a part in causing instability. )/E(The realistic simulation of crowds is of great importance to the design of safe structures and urban environments. Within structural engineering, crowd behaviour is often overlooked when considering the dynamics of structures such as floors and bridges which are designed to carry human traffic. Predicting the vibration response of lightweight, slender structures to crowds has been an active area of study since the closure of the London Millennium Bridge in 2000 due to uncomfortable vibrations under crowded conditions. There are still gaps in the knowledge in this area, including the exact extent to which gait synchronisation plays a part in causing instability. )/Pg 41 0 R/K[0 1]>>
endobj

531 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 155 0 R/T(In this poster we examine an agent-based approach to crowd simulation originally proposed by Moussa\357d et al. which aims to model human behaviour realistically through vision-based decision making. The model has pedestrians adapt their direction and speed based on a simple procedure fed by visual information. )/E(In this poster we examine an agent-based approach to crowd simulation originally proposed by Moussa\357d et al. which aims to model human behaviour realistically through vision-based decision making. The model has pedestrians adapt their direction and speed based on a simple procedure fed by visual information. )/Pg 41 0 R/K[2]>>
endobj

532 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 155 0 R/T(We first outline the procedure and observe its effectiveness at reproducing real-world behaviours compared to other approaches.  We review a modification to the model which includes the desire of some pedestrians to maintain cohesive \217social groups\220. This is relevant to human-structure interaction as there is evidence that such groups are more likely to synchronise their footsteps. )/E(We first outline the procedure and observe its effectiveness at reproducing real-world behaviours compared to other approaches.  We review a modification to the model which includes the desire of some pedestrians to maintain cohesive \217social groups\220. This is relevant to human-structure interaction as there is evidence that such groups are more likely to synchronise their footsteps. )/Pg 41 0 R/K[3 4]>>
endobj

