557 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 159 0 R/T(Airborne transmission of pathogens in hospital patient rooms remains a significant concern for infection prevention and control. While ventilation design and respiratory emissions have been widely studied, less is understood about the transient release and resuspension of bioaerosols associated with routine patient room activities such as bed making, curtain movement, and surface cleaning.)/E(Airborne transmission of pathogens in hospital patient rooms remains a significant concern for infection prevention and control. While ventilation design and respiratory emissions have been widely studied, less is understood about the transient release and resuspension of bioaerosols associated with routine patient room activities such as bed making, curtain movement, and surface cleaning.)/Pg 45 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

558 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 159 0 R/T(This PhD project investigates activity-induced airborne contamination through an integrated experimental and computational approach. The research involves controlled chamber experiments that replicate realistic care scenarios under mechanical ventilation. Each activity will be tested individually under varying ventilation, with repeated trials to capture both the generation and post-activity decay of airborne contaminants. Particle concentrations and viable microbial content will be measured using optical particle counters and microbial air samplers to assess spatial and temporal dispersion patterns.)/E(This PhD project investigates activity-induced airborne contamination through an integrated experimental and computational approach. The research involves controlled chamber experiments that replicate realistic care scenarios under mechanical ventilation. Each activity will be tested individually under varying ventilation, with repeated trials to capture both the generation and post-activity decay of airborne contaminants. Particle concentrations and viable microbial content will be measured using optical particle counters and microbial air samplers to assess spatial and temporal dispersion patterns.)/Pg 45 0 R/K[1 2]>>
endobj

559 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 159 0 R/T(In parallel, Computational Fluid Dynamics \(CFD\) models will be developed to simulate airflow, particle transport, and deposition associated with these activities. Turbulence effects will be explored using appropriate modelling approaches, and CFD predictions will be validated against the experimental data. Particular emphasis will be placed on particle size distributions relevant to viable pathogen transport and occupant exposure risk.)/E(In parallel, Computational Fluid Dynamics \(CFD\) models will be developed to simulate airflow, particle transport, and deposition associated with these activities. Turbulence effects will be explored using appropriate modelling approaches, and CFD predictions will be validated against the experimental data. Particular emphasis will be placed on particle size distributions relevant to viable pathogen transport and occupant exposure risk.)/Pg 45 0 R/K[3 4]>>
endobj

560 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 159 0 R/T(This dual-method approach aims to clarify the physical mechanisms behind short-term airborne contamination and identify high-risk care activities that may require mitigation through targeted ventilation or procedural adjustments. The project contributes to the HumanIC network\220s objective of improving hospital environmental safety through human-centric indoor climate design, as part of a Marie Sklodowska-Curie funded doctoral programme.)/E(This dual-method approach aims to clarify the physical mechanisms behind short-term airborne contamination and identify high-risk care activities that may require mitigation through targeted ventilation or procedural adjustments. The project contributes to the HumanIC network\220s objective of improving hospital environmental safety through human-centric indoor climate design, as part of a Marie Sklodowska-Curie funded doctoral programme.)/Pg 45 0 R/K[5 6]>>
endobj

561 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H3/P 159 0 R/T(Currently in its early stage, the project focuses on literature review, formulation of research questions, CFD model setup, and experimental protocol development. The outcomes are expected to support the development of evidence-based, activity-specific infection control and ventilation strategies in healthcare environments.)/E(Currently in its early stage, the project focuses on literature review, formulation of research questions, CFD model setup, and experimental protocol development. The outcomes are expected to support the development of evidence-based, activity-specific infection control and ventilation strategies in healthcare environments.)/Pg 45 0 R/K[7]>>
endobj

